1
00:00:03,270 --> 00:00:05,770
So what kind of cruise is this you're going on?

2
00:00:05,770 --> 00:00:08,110
It's called the Born Again Boat Ride.

3
00:00:09,010 --> 00:00:11,880
Christian Quarterly gave it their highest rating--

4
00:00:11,880 --> 00:00:14,380
five thorny crowns.

5
00:00:14,380 --> 00:00:16,780
I do wish you'd come with me, Sheldon.

6
00:00:16,780 --> 00:00:20,650
Well, Mom, if I did, it would be conclusive proof

7
00:00:20,650 --> 00:00:22,850
that your God can work miracles.

8
00:00:23,890 --> 00:00:24,960
You're missing out.

9
00:00:24,960 --> 00:00:26,290
It's gonna be wall-to-wall fun.

10
00:00:26,290 --> 00:00:27,430
It's all themed.

11
00:00:27,430 --> 00:00:29,530
There's Jonah and the Whale watching.

12
00:00:30,930 --> 00:00:33,900
All-you-can-eat Last Supper Buffet.

13
00:00:33,900 --> 00:00:36,300
And my personal favorite, Gunning with God.

14
00:00:36,300 --> 00:00:39,800
What's Gunning with God? I'm afraid to ask.

15
00:00:39,810 --> 00:00:42,510
Oh, it is a hoot and a half.

16
00:00:42,510 --> 00:00:45,140
You write your sins on a clay pigeon,

17
00:00:45,150 --> 00:00:46,310
they fire 'em up in the air,

18
00:00:46,310 --> 00:00:49,610
and you pulverize them with a 12-gauge shotgun

19
00:00:49,820 --> 00:00:52,780
full of our Lord's forgiveness.

20
00:00:52,780 --> 00:00:55,090
Frankly, Mom, I'm encouraged to see

21
00:00:55,090 --> 00:00:56,860
how advanced your group has become--

22
00:00:56,860 --> 00:00:58,590
you are willing to sail out into the ocean

23
00:00:58,590 --> 00:01:01,490
without fear of falling off the edge.

24
00:01:01,490 --> 00:01:03,290
For example,

25
00:01:03,300 --> 00:01:05,230
if Shelly was aboard, he'd write,

26
00:01:05,230 --> 00:01:07,300
"Smart mouth" on his pigeon,

27
00:01:07,300 --> 00:01:11,240
and then bam!

28
00:01:11,240 --> 00:01:13,640
The Lord giveth and the Lord bloweth away.

29
00:01:14,140 --> 00:01:17,940
Well, Mom, according to my itinerary for our weekend together,

30
00:01:17,940 --> 00:01:21,150
- the fun begins with fried chicken. - Sounds delicious.

31
00:01:21,150 --> 00:01:23,150
Good, 'cause I got you everything you need to make it.

32
00:01:24,250 --> 00:01:26,080
You are in for a treat.

33
00:01:26,090 --> 00:01:27,050
My mother's fried chicken

34
00:01:27,050 --> 00:01:30,960
is why we had to buy my dad the extra large coffin.

35
00:01:31,360 --> 00:01:33,620
Sheldon, she just got off the plane.

36
00:01:33,630 --> 00:01:34,360
She doesn't want to cook.

37
00:01:34,360 --> 00:01:35,960
Of course she does.

38
00:01:35,960 --> 00:01:38,530
Making me food is her way of saying "I love you""

39
00:01:38,530 --> 00:01:40,800
Making me food when she's too tired to cook

40
00:01:40,800 --> 00:01:42,870
is her way of saying "I really love you."

41
00:01:42,870 --> 00:01:45,970
Actually, I wouldn't mind going out for a bite, Sheldon.

42
00:01:46,070 --> 00:01:47,470
Won't that spoil our appetites

43
00:01:47,470 --> 00:01:48,810
for the chicken you're going to make me?

44
00:01:48,810 --> 00:01:50,680
All right, that settles it, we're going out.

45
00:01:50,680 --> 00:01:52,810
Do you like sushi? There's a great little place down the street.

46
00:01:52,810 --> 00:01:54,150
I've never had it,

47
00:01:54,150 --> 00:01:57,150
but there's no harm in trying something new.

48
00:01:57,450 --> 00:02:00,050
There's a lot of harm in trying something new.

49
00:02:00,050 --> 00:02:03,650
That's why we test out drugs and cosmetics on bunny rabbits.

50
00:02:03,960 --> 00:02:06,560
Sheldon, you're talking like a crazy person.

51
00:02:06,560 --> 00:02:09,860
Actually, I had him tested as a child.

52
00:02:09,860 --> 00:02:11,630
Doctor says he's fine.

53
00:02:12,530 --> 00:02:13,230
Told you.

54
00:02:13,230 --> 00:02:14,870
Although, I do regret not following up

55
00:02:14,870 --> 00:02:17,100
with that specialist in Houston.

56
00:02:43,990 --> 00:02:45,550
いらっしゃいます

57
00:02:45,560 --> 00:02:46,720
Stop yelling!

58
00:02:48,030 --> 00:02:50,130
I'm not happy about this.

59
00:02:50,130 --> 00:02:52,530
What's the last thing you were ever happy about?

60
00:02:52,630 --> 00:02:55,460
The prospect of fried chicken.

61
00:02:56,870 --> 00:02:58,430
This is exciting.

62
00:02:58,440 --> 00:03:01,040
Back home, the diner on Route Four serves sushi,

63
00:03:01,040 --> 00:03:03,110
but it's just cut up fish sticks

64
00:03:03,110 --> 00:03:04,710
and a side of Uncle Ben's.

65
00:03:05,110 --> 00:03:08,370
They put it on the menu in those kung fu letters,

66
00:03:08,380 --> 00:03:10,480
but that don't make it sushi.

67
00:03:11,080 --> 00:03:15,420
Uh, kung fu letters might not be politically correct.

68
00:03:15,420 --> 00:03:18,890
Oh, I thought the one we couldn't say was "ching chong"

69
00:03:19,590 --> 00:03:22,360
Yeah, yeah, that, too.

70
00:03:22,760 --> 00:03:24,160
So, Shelly,

71
00:03:24,160 --> 00:03:26,090
what's up with you and your friend Amy,

72
00:03:26,100 --> 00:03:28,530
if you don't mind a mother prying a bit?

73
00:03:28,530 --> 00:03:31,600
Well, there's actually big news on the Amy front.

74
00:03:31,600 --> 00:03:32,440
She's been studying

75
00:03:32,440 --> 00:03:35,440
the neurobiology of addiction in lower animals.

76
00:03:35,440 --> 00:03:39,610
She's this close to getting a starfish hooked on cocaine.

77
00:03:40,110 --> 00:03:42,910
Do you have any idea what's going on with those two?

78
00:03:42,910 --> 00:03:44,810
It's kind of like the Loch Ness monster.

79
00:03:44,820 --> 00:03:46,620
Maybe there's something there, maybe there isn't.

80
00:03:46,620 --> 00:03:48,320
We'll probably never know.

81
00:03:49,420 --> 00:03:53,620
But sometimes it's fun to creep yourself out thinking about it.

82
00:03:56,460 --> 00:03:59,760
How are you doing on the young lady front?

83
00:03:59,760 --> 00:04:02,630
I hear you're in some sort of a long distance situation?

84
00:04:02,630 --> 00:04:04,800
Yeah, it's Raj's sister.

85
00:04:04,800 --> 00:04:06,870
It's kind of tough.  She's in India.

86
00:04:06,870 --> 00:04:09,570
Also, her parents aren't happy she's dating someone white.

87
00:04:09,570 --> 00:04:11,870
Oh, that's a funny turn, isn't it?

88
00:04:12,170 --> 00:04:16,040
You never think about it going the other way.

89
00:04:18,780 --> 00:04:20,750
Well, you can't force things.

90
00:04:20,750 --> 00:04:23,420
You need to figure out if you're in a relationship

91
00:04:23,420 --> 00:04:24,350
or if you're just calling it one.

92
00:04:24,350 --> 00:04:27,030
It's like they say--a cat can have kittens in the oven

93
00:04:27,030 --> 00:04:29,130
but that don't make 'em biscuits.

94
00:04:30,430 --> 00:04:32,060
And that reminds me of another saying.

95
00:04:32,060 --> 00:04:34,160
You can lead a chicken to Crisco,

96
00:04:34,160 --> 00:04:36,660
but you can't make your mother fry it.

97
00:04:38,870 --> 00:04:41,570
Sheldon, you pester me one more time about chicken,

98
00:04:41,570 --> 00:04:44,970
I will put you over my knee right here in this restaurant.

99
00:04:46,470 --> 00:04:49,140
Please pester her. Please, for me.

100
00:04:52,310 --> 00:04:54,550
So, Mrs. Cooper, what did you think of the sushi?

101
00:04:54,550 --> 00:04:55,520
It was good.

102
00:04:55,520 --> 00:04:57,020
The only thing that would have made it better

103
00:04:57,020 --> 00:04:59,490
is if it was cooked

104
00:05:00,290 --> 00:05:02,890
and if it was beef.

105
00:05:03,690 --> 00:05:06,760
Sheldon, when is your landlord going to fix the elevator?

106
00:05:06,760 --> 00:05:08,500
I don't know.

107
00:05:08,500 --> 00:05:10,130
Lately we've been talking about

108
00:05:10,130 --> 00:05:13,670
converting it into a missile silo.

109
00:05:14,570 --> 00:05:15,340
Your son seems to think

110
00:05:15,340 --> 00:05:18,970
we need to launch a preemptive strike on Burbank.

111
00:05:19,380 --> 00:05:22,180
Get them before they get us.

112
00:05:28,520 --> 00:05:31,950
Hey, look who decided to show up.

113
00:05:32,560 --> 00:05:34,020
Raj, what are you doing?

114
00:05:34,020 --> 00:05:38,190
I couldn't find you guys so I bought six new friends.

115
00:05:39,230 --> 00:05:42,600
Three, sadly, are dead.

116
00:05:42,800 --> 00:05:45,070
Mom, you remember Rajesh?

117
00:05:45,070 --> 00:05:47,640
- Rajesh, my mother. - Of course.Mrs. Cooper.

118
00:05:47,640 --> 00:05:49,370
So nice to see you again.

119
00:05:49,370 --> 00:05:51,770
Well, it's so nice to see you, too.

120
00:05:54,240 --> 00:05:56,140
I thought it was our Indians

121
00:05:56,150 --> 00:05:59,310
that had the occasional alcohol problem.

122
00:06:01,180 --> 00:06:03,550
We don't say that, either.

123
00:06:04,750 --> 00:06:06,760
I'll make you a list.

124
00:06:06,760 --> 00:06:09,420
Oh, that would be mighty white of you.

125
00:06:11,530 --> 00:06:15,260
So, Raj, what pain are you trying

126
00:06:15,270 --> 00:06:17,270
to cover up with alcohol?

127
00:06:17,270 --> 00:06:18,570
Nothing, I'm fine.

128
00:06:18,570 --> 00:06:20,800
- Are ya? - No.

129
00:06:21,600 --> 00:06:22,400
That's better.

130
00:06:22,410 --> 00:06:24,140
Now tell me what's bothering you.

131
00:06:24,140 --> 00:06:26,240
I'm so lonely.

132
00:06:26,240 --> 00:06:28,840
Oh, yes, born alone, die alone.

133
00:06:28,850 --> 00:06:30,880
It's a tragic human condition.

134
00:06:30,880 --> 00:06:32,180
Now, Raj,

135
00:06:32,180 --> 00:06:33,450
if you'll excuse my mother,

136
00:06:33,450 --> 00:06:35,350
she's about to make a pecan pie

137
00:06:35,350 --> 00:06:36,280
that'll be so good

138
00:06:36,280 --> 00:06:39,420
I'll almost forget how she blew it with the fried chicken.

139
00:06:39,420 --> 00:06:41,460
Sheldon, your friend is hurtin'.

140
00:06:41,460 --> 00:06:43,560
What do we do when someone's hurtin'?

141
00:06:43,760 --> 00:06:46,530
Offer them a hot beverage.

142
00:06:46,530 --> 00:06:48,030
And when they're drunk as a skunk,

143
00:06:48,030 --> 00:06:49,400
what beverage do we offer?

144
00:06:49,400 --> 00:06:51,530
Coffee.

145
00:06:51,530 --> 00:06:53,830
And what do we do it with?

146
00:07:01,140 --> 00:07:03,380
Now you listen to me.

147
00:07:03,380 --> 00:07:06,450
I know you feel like you can't find someone,

148
00:07:06,450 --> 00:07:08,280
but there's a lock for every key.

149
00:07:08,280 --> 00:07:10,780
Back home, there's a girl works at the Wal-Mart.

150
00:07:10,790 --> 00:07:12,920
Tall, tall girl.

151
00:07:12,920 --> 00:07:16,020
Woman could hunt geese with a rake.

152
00:07:16,230 --> 00:07:18,420
Thought she'd never find a man,

153
00:07:18,430 --> 00:07:20,130
then one day, wouldn't ya know,

154
00:07:20,130 --> 00:07:23,560
Harlem Globetrotters come to town.

155
00:07:23,570 --> 00:07:25,670
Long story short,

156
00:07:25,670 --> 00:07:27,870
today that woman travels the world

157
00:07:27,870 --> 00:07:29,770
with a semi-professional basketball player

158
00:07:29,770 --> 00:07:32,940
and two beautiful mixed-race babies.

159
00:07:33,640 --> 00:07:37,250
I didn't get a lot of that because of your accent,

160
00:07:37,250 --> 00:07:39,650
but the general tone was soothing

161
00:07:39,650 --> 00:07:41,250
and somehow I feel better.

162
00:07:42,450 --> 00:07:46,390
I'm not going to get my pecan pie, am I?

163
00:07:46,790 --> 00:07:48,560
You want some Oreos?

164
00:07:48,560 --> 00:07:50,160
- Double Stuf? - No, regular.

165
00:07:50,160 --> 00:07:52,830
Nice. Kick a man when he's down.

166
00:07:54,100 --> 00:07:57,000
I'm glad we're finally getting to do something together,

167
00:07:57,000 --> 00:07:58,870
- just the two of us.  - Sure.

168
00:07:58,870 --> 00:08:02,000
One thing you really miss when you're on vacation is laundry.

169
00:08:02,310 --> 00:08:04,370
Careful, you're using too much Downey.

170
00:08:04,370 --> 00:08:07,440
You know if my clothes get too soft it makes me sleepy.

171
00:08:07,440 --> 00:08:09,610
Well, this takes me back.

172
00:08:09,610 --> 00:08:12,610
Me doing your laundry, you next to me criticizing.

173
00:08:12,620 --> 00:08:14,620
It is nice, isn't it?

174
00:08:15,820 --> 00:08:17,310
Mrs. Cooper.

175
00:08:17,320 --> 00:08:20,510
Oh, hello, darlin'.

176
00:08:20,520 --> 00:08:22,860
Sheldon, you didn't tell me your mom was coming.

177
00:08:23,060 --> 00:08:25,560
It was in my weekly e-mail blast.

178
00:08:25,760 --> 00:08:28,300
Right between "Beet season is finally here"

179
00:08:28,300 --> 00:08:32,830
and "Uh-oh, red stool from beets leads to cancer scare."

180
00:08:35,900 --> 00:08:37,610
So, how've you been?

181
00:08:37,610 --> 00:08:38,740
Good, good.

182
00:08:38,740 --> 00:08:40,340
I hear that Leonard has a new girlfriend.

183
00:08:40,340 --> 00:08:41,880
How are you doing with all that?

184
00:08:41,880 --> 00:08:43,880
Oh, fine. You know, it's been a while.

185
00:08:43,880 --> 00:08:45,610
I'm getting back out there.

186
00:08:45,620 --> 00:08:47,880
Let me ask you, when you get back out there,

187
00:08:47,880 --> 00:08:50,480
are you wearing this?

188
00:08:51,420 --> 00:08:53,920
Well, it's super cute on.

189
00:08:53,920 --> 00:08:55,790
That top has paid for itself in free drinks

190
00:08:55,790 --> 00:08:58,390
like ten times what it cost.

191
00:08:58,390 --> 00:09:02,260
Yes, Penny has a lot of her money tied up in promiscuity futures.

192
00:09:03,470 --> 00:09:05,870
Hon, you think maybe the reason

193
00:09:05,870 --> 00:09:08,900
why you're having trouble finding a guy to settle down with

194
00:09:08,910 --> 00:09:11,110
is because you're letting them ride the roller coaster

195
00:09:11,110 --> 00:09:13,310
without buying a ticket?

196
00:09:13,910 --> 00:09:17,350
Oh, they don't always get to ride the roller coaster.

197
00:09:17,350 --> 00:09:21,220
Sometimes they only get to spin the teacups.

198
00:09:24,190 --> 00:09:25,350
Now I'm going out tonight.

199
00:09:25,350 --> 00:09:26,760
Would it be crazy to ask you

200
00:09:26,760 --> 00:09:28,390
to look at the outfit I'm going to wear?

201
00:09:28,390 --> 00:09:30,290
Oh, not crazy at all.

202
00:09:30,290 --> 00:09:31,230
And don't beat yourself up.

203
00:09:31,230 --> 00:09:33,460
When I was your age, you could have me for a car ride

204
00:09:33,460 --> 00:09:36,030
and a bottle of strawberry wine.

205
00:09:40,700 --> 00:09:44,340
That will not be in this week's e-mail blast.

206
00:09:45,610 --> 00:09:46,870
So, this spring,

207
00:09:46,880 --> 00:09:50,240
I get to go to the International Space Station.

208
00:09:50,250 --> 00:09:54,120
Oh, my word, a trip to the heavens.

209
00:09:55,380 --> 00:09:57,620
If you ever want to live there eternally,

210
00:09:57,620 --> 00:09:59,890
I've got a good book you could read.

211
00:09:59,890 --> 00:10:03,290
Thanks, but I watch the Charlie Brown Christmas special every year,

212
00:10:03,290 --> 00:10:04,760
so I get the gist.

213
00:10:04,760 --> 00:10:07,600
- I bet your mom is really proud of you. - Nope.

214
00:10:07,600 --> 00:10:09,230
She says if I don't back out

215
00:10:09,230 --> 00:10:11,930
she's going to go on a hunger strike.

216
00:10:11,930 --> 00:10:12,870
It would take years before

217
00:10:12,870 --> 00:10:15,900
she'd be in any kind of danger, but still.

218
00:10:16,110 --> 00:10:18,940
I've got a treat for us tomorrow, Mom.

219
00:10:18,940 --> 00:10:20,240
I'm taking you

220
00:10:20,240 --> 00:10:22,980
to see Saul Perlmutter give a lecture

221
00:10:22,980 --> 00:10:24,980
about his Nobel Prize-winning work in cosmology.

222
00:10:24,980 --> 00:10:26,310
And the best part is,

223
00:10:26,320 --> 00:10:28,320
at the Q and A afterward,

224
00:10:28,320 --> 00:10:29,850
I've worked up a couple of "Q's"

225
00:10:29,850 --> 00:10:32,520
that will stump his sorry "A."

226
00:10:34,190 --> 00:10:35,090
I don't know, Shelly.

227
00:10:35,090 --> 00:10:37,460
I thought we could do a little sightseeing.

228
00:10:37,460 --> 00:10:39,930
What sight is better than your little boy

229
00:10:39,930 --> 00:10:42,530
embarrassing a Nobel laureate?

230
00:10:42,530 --> 00:10:44,570
Come on, Sheldon, we'll take your mom

231
00:10:44,570 --> 00:10:46,300
to see the Hollywood sign,

232
00:10:46,300 --> 00:10:48,140
the wax museum, the Walk of Fame.

233
00:10:48,140 --> 00:10:50,200
Ooh, maybe a little Rodeo Drive.

234
00:10:50,210 --> 00:10:53,070
Well, I can't spend $12,000 on a handbag,

235
00:10:53,080 --> 00:10:54,940
but it's free to look upon those

236
00:10:54,940 --> 00:10:57,510
who do with righteous condemnation.

237
00:10:59,350 --> 00:11:00,680
What do you say?

238
00:11:00,680 --> 00:11:02,850
What do I say?

239
00:11:02,850 --> 00:11:05,590
I say you people need to stop ruining my mom's visit

240
00:11:05,590 --> 00:11:08,290
with your sushi, and your sadness

241
00:11:08,290 --> 00:11:09,490
and your slutty shirts.

242
00:11:09,490 --> 00:11:11,060
Stop it.

243
00:11:15,030 --> 00:11:18,500
He's not talking about your shirt. Your shirt is fine.

244
00:11:22,310 --> 00:11:23,910
These are delicious.

245
00:11:23,910 --> 00:11:26,210
The trick to pancakes is bacon grease.

246
00:11:26,220 --> 00:11:28,520
I cook everything in it.

247
00:11:28,920 --> 00:11:31,350
Everything? Aren't you worried about your health?

248
00:11:31,350 --> 00:11:33,720
Oh, doctors are always changing their mind.

249
00:11:33,720 --> 00:11:35,420
One week bacon grease is bad for you.

250
00:11:35,430 --> 00:11:38,090
The next week we're not getting enough of it.

251
00:11:39,260 --> 00:11:41,560
Good morning, Shelly.

252
00:11:41,560 --> 00:11:45,530
Mom, I want to apologize for my behavior last night.

253
00:11:45,540 --> 00:11:47,300
- Apology accepted.  - Great.

254
00:11:47,300 --> 00:11:49,840
Now, you're going to love the Perlmutter lecture.

255
00:11:49,840 --> 00:11:53,910
Look, he will be stating that the universe is older than 6,000 years,

256
00:11:53,910 --> 00:11:56,180
but I thought you could stick your fingers in your ears

257
00:11:56,180 --> 00:11:58,950
and hum "Amazing Grace" during those parts.

258
00:11:58,950 --> 00:12:01,680
I am still going out with your friends.

259
00:12:01,680 --> 00:12:03,920
But I apologized.

260
00:12:03,920 --> 00:12:07,560
And that was hard for me because I didn't do anything wrong.

261
00:12:09,590 --> 00:12:13,390
Shelly, I hung out with you in enough dusty lecture halls

262
00:12:13,400 --> 00:12:14,700
while you were growing up.

263
00:12:14,700 --> 00:12:16,560
I want to go sightseeing.

264
00:12:16,570 --> 00:12:18,000
So why don't you have some pancakes,

265
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:19,630
get dressed and come with us.

266
00:12:19,640 --> 00:12:22,770
I'm not going, and you can't make me.

267
00:12:22,770 --> 00:12:26,640
You're right, I can't. Have a nice day.

268
00:12:27,440 --> 00:12:29,410
Well, I'm going to stand here until you change your mind.

269
00:12:29,410 --> 00:12:31,680
Well, then you are going to stand there all day.

270
00:12:33,880 --> 00:12:38,550
I'm just gonna take my bacon grease and slide over there.

271
00:12:38,550 --> 00:12:41,620
I can't believe my own mother is abandoning me.

272
00:12:41,620 --> 00:12:43,890
I am not abandoning you.

273
00:12:43,890 --> 00:12:46,090
Sheldon, abandoning you is leaving you in a basket

274
00:12:46,100 --> 00:12:47,460
on a church doorstep.

275
00:12:47,460 --> 00:12:52,130
I am going to Hollywood and thank a wax Ronald Reagan

276
00:12:52,140 --> 00:12:54,440
for his service to our country.

277
00:12:56,540 --> 00:12:59,170
We appear to be at a crossroads in our relationship, Mother.

278
00:12:59,180 --> 00:13:00,810
Well, I guess we are.

279
00:13:00,810 --> 00:13:02,880
Sorry. Syrup.

280
00:13:03,780 --> 00:13:05,350
All right, Mom.

281
00:13:05,350 --> 00:13:08,320
When you're at the Ripley's Believe It Or Not Museum,

282
00:13:08,320 --> 00:13:11,720
if they have an exhibit about a mother who threw away a chance

283
00:13:11,720 --> 00:13:13,890
to spend the day with the world's most wonderful son,

284
00:13:13,890 --> 00:13:15,690
believe it, because it's true.

285
00:13:18,190 --> 00:13:20,530
I hadn't...

286
00:13:21,830 --> 00:13:24,530
That lecture was a waste of time.

287
00:13:24,530 --> 00:13:26,530
I made more accurate diagrams

288
00:13:26,540 --> 00:13:29,070
of the expansion of the early universe on the nursery wall

289
00:13:29,070 --> 00:13:31,070
with the contents of my diaper.

290
00:13:33,010 --> 00:13:34,680
Are you getting sick?

291
00:13:34,680 --> 00:13:37,040
No, I'm just allergic to people

292
00:13:37,050 --> 00:13:39,880
who get Nobel Prizes for no good reason.

293
00:13:39,880 --> 00:13:42,980
Sheldon, is it possible that your foul mood--

294
00:13:42,990 --> 00:13:46,690
or, to use the clinical term, bitchiness--

295
00:13:47,290 --> 00:13:50,120
is because your mother isn't making you a priority?

296
00:13:50,130 --> 00:13:55,100
No. Or to use the clinical term, "nuh-uh""

297
00:13:56,160 --> 00:13:58,130
Are you sure? The infant-mother pair-bond

298
00:13:58,130 --> 00:13:59,470
is the building block of primate psychology.

299
00:13:59,470 --> 00:14:00,630
Oh, there it is.

300
00:14:00,640 --> 00:14:02,640
It always comes back to monkeys with you.

301
00:14:02,640 --> 00:14:05,570
Just monkeys, monkeys, monkeys.

302
00:14:05,680 --> 00:14:07,940
Sheldon, we're all animals.

303
00:14:07,940 --> 00:14:10,480
And granted, there are aspects of you that are extraordinary,

304
00:14:10,480 --> 00:14:12,850
but when it comes to emotions and relationships,

305
00:14:12,850 --> 00:14:14,250
you're just like everybody else.

306
00:14:14,250 --> 00:14:17,350
Are you trying to suggest that my emotional problems

307
00:14:17,350 --> 00:14:20,790
are no different than those of a stupid person?

308
00:14:21,090 --> 00:14:23,120
Actually, some research indicates

309
00:14:23,130 --> 00:14:24,460
that by not overthinking,

310
00:14:24,460 --> 00:14:26,590
the less intelligent handle emotions better.

311
00:14:29,500 --> 00:14:31,170
Sure you're not coming down with a cold?

312
00:14:31,170 --> 00:14:33,230
Oh, yes, the common cold.

313
00:14:33,240 --> 00:14:34,400
Just like everyone else.

314
00:14:34,400 --> 00:14:35,640
You'd love that, wouldn't you?

315
00:14:38,070 --> 00:14:41,010
Oh, this one's sweet.

316
00:14:41,010 --> 00:14:43,750
You know, for your rosary rattlers.

317
00:14:44,450 --> 00:14:49,950
Mrs. Cooper, we say "Catholics" not "rosary rattlers".

318
00:14:49,950 --> 00:14:52,240
My goodness, it's a wonder you people in California

319
00:14:52,240 --> 00:14:53,450
can talk at all.

320
00:14:54,720 --> 00:14:56,690
This is like the worst Hollywood tour ever.

321
00:14:56,690 --> 00:14:58,760
What are we gonna do? She wanted to see churches.

322
00:14:58,760 --> 00:15:01,500
Hey, they have wine here, don't they?

323
00:15:04,070 --> 00:15:07,740
None of our gods have abs like that.

324
00:15:07,740 --> 00:15:10,870
Yep, that's the last Jew who did sit-ups.

325
00:15:11,840 --> 00:15:14,310
And look where it got him.

326
00:15:15,840 --> 00:15:18,850
Hey, while we're here, why don't we all do some praying?

327
00:15:18,850 --> 00:15:20,880
Let's put a little church in this church.

328
00:15:20,880 --> 00:15:22,420
Oh, I'm not sure we should...

329
00:15:22,420 --> 00:15:24,520
It's easy. I'll show you how.

330
00:15:24,520 --> 00:15:26,190
Lord, Mary Cooper here.

331
00:15:26,190 --> 00:15:29,590
Coming to you from Gomorrah, California.

332
00:15:29,590 --> 00:15:31,330
I want to thank you

333
00:15:31,330 --> 00:15:34,030
for the blessing that is my little Shelly.

334
00:15:34,030 --> 00:15:36,960
I also want to thank you for the continued strength

335
00:15:36,970 --> 00:15:39,930
not to coldcock him with my Bible.

336
00:15:41,300 --> 00:15:43,700
All right, Penny, your turn.

337
00:15:43,710 --> 00:15:45,470
Okay, um...

338
00:15:45,470 --> 00:15:46,470
Hey, God.

339
00:15:46,480 --> 00:15:49,210
What's up?

340
00:15:49,210 --> 00:15:50,950
Um, I'm good, but, uh,

341
00:15:50,950 --> 00:15:52,650
it would be a big help to my family

342
00:15:52,650 --> 00:15:54,850
if you could get my brother to stop cooking meth.

343
00:15:57,490 --> 00:16:00,190
But no cops. Be cool.

344
00:16:01,920 --> 00:16:04,290
She also goes a little overboard

345
00:16:04,300 --> 00:16:05,630
on the "love thy neighbor" .

346
00:16:05,630 --> 00:16:09,160
Could probably use that chat you had with Mary Magdalene.

347
00:16:09,570 --> 00:16:10,570
Leonard, you're up.

348
00:16:10,570 --> 00:16:12,530
Wasserman, you're on deck.

349
00:16:13,040 --> 00:16:14,840
Okay. I don't know...

350
00:16:14,840 --> 00:16:19,310
it's probably a little late to ask you to make me taller.

351
00:16:23,510 --> 00:16:26,510
Oh, um...if you could help out with me and my girlfriend.

352
00:16:26,520 --> 00:16:28,650
She's all the way in India. That would be great.

353
00:16:28,650 --> 00:16:30,020
Hear that? Girl trouble.

354
00:16:30,020 --> 00:16:32,990
Turns out we were both wrong on that front.

355
00:16:36,020 --> 00:16:37,190
- How about you?  - Oh, me?

356
00:16:37,190 --> 00:16:38,560
No. Thanks, I'm good.

357
00:16:38,560 --> 00:16:42,230
I'm really just trying not to burst into flames.

358
00:16:45,000 --> 00:16:46,370
Rajesh?

359
00:16:49,040 --> 00:16:50,810
He says he's having trouble

360
00:16:50,810 --> 00:16:52,940
dropping those last five pounds.

361
00:16:52,940 --> 00:16:57,140
Huh, I might have gone with the talking-to-girls thing.

362
00:16:58,480 --> 00:17:00,350
No, you only get one wish.

363
00:17:18,170 --> 00:17:21,170
Look at the two of us.

364
00:17:21,170 --> 00:17:24,200
Me, a highly regarded physicist.

365
00:17:24,210 --> 00:17:26,340
The kind of mind that comes along once,

366
00:17:26,340 --> 00:17:28,380
maybe twice in a generation.

367
00:17:28,380 --> 00:17:30,880
You, the common man,

368
00:17:30,880 --> 00:17:34,520
tired from your labors as a stockbroker,

369
00:17:34,520 --> 00:17:38,520
or vacuum cleaner salesman, or bootblack.

370
00:17:40,120 --> 00:17:42,860
But deep down inside...

371
00:17:42,860 --> 00:17:45,430
apparently we're just two peas in a pod.

372
00:17:45,430 --> 00:17:48,600
A regular pea,

373
00:17:48,600 --> 00:17:51,200
and the kind of pea that comes along once,

374
00:17:51,200 --> 00:17:53,430
maybe twice in a generation.

375
00:17:57,810 --> 00:17:58,970
Rain.

376
00:17:58,980 --> 00:18:01,480
Another great equalizer.

377
00:18:01,480 --> 00:18:03,310
Falling on the head of the brilliant

378
00:18:03,310 --> 00:18:05,310
and the unremarkable alike.

379
00:18:12,490 --> 00:18:14,860
Smarty-pants.

380
00:18:18,160 --> 00:18:20,490
Oh, Mrs. Cooper, it smells so good.

381
00:18:20,500 --> 00:18:21,830
You take notes, darlin'.

382
00:18:21,830 --> 00:18:24,160
The real way to get a man is with melted cheese

383
00:18:24,170 --> 00:18:26,030
and cream of mushroom soup.

384
00:18:26,040 --> 00:18:29,500
He'll die at 50 but his love will be true.

385
00:18:34,340 --> 00:18:36,410
I need a tissue. This one got wet.

386
00:18:39,710 --> 00:18:40,950
Here.

387
00:18:40,950 --> 00:18:44,450
Thank you. Yeah, I've learned something today.

388
00:18:44,450 --> 00:18:48,360
You and I, in so many ways,

389
00:18:48,360 --> 00:18:49,830
other than intelligence and what counts,

390
00:18:49,830 --> 00:18:51,630
we're the same

391
00:18:53,900 --> 00:18:57,200
- Sweetheart, are you sick? - I hope so.

392
00:18:57,200 --> 00:18:59,470
because if this is well, life isn't worth living.

393
00:18:59,470 --> 00:19:02,770
Oh, sugarpie, you are burning up.

394
00:19:02,770 --> 00:19:05,010
- We've got to get you to bed.  - Okay.

395
00:19:05,010 --> 00:19:07,470
Don't worry. Mama's here to take care of her baby.

396
00:19:07,480 --> 00:19:11,510
And just to be clear, only her baby

397
00:19:11,510 --> 00:19:12,810
and not these other people.

398
00:19:12,820 --> 00:19:14,820
Of course.

399
00:19:14,820 --> 00:19:16,450
Can I have tea with honey

400
00:19:16,450 --> 00:19:18,150
and toast with the crust cut off?

401
00:19:18,150 --> 00:19:20,820
You can have whatever you want.

402
00:19:20,820 --> 00:19:23,560
Thanks, Mom. You're the best.

403
00:19:30,810 --> 00:19:33,470
Boy, last time I put VapoRub on you,

404
00:19:33,480 --> 00:19:36,380
you didn't have hair on your chest.

405
00:19:36,380 --> 00:19:39,580
I know, it filled in last year.

406
00:19:41,820 --> 00:19:44,650
I didn't get to spend a lot of time with you on this visit.

407
00:19:44,650 --> 00:19:46,650
And whose fault was that?

408
00:19:47,590 --> 00:19:49,620
Yours.

409
00:19:50,990 --> 00:19:53,160
Shelly, you're not eight years old anymore.

410
00:19:53,160 --> 00:19:55,030
We have to have a different relationship.

411
00:19:55,030 --> 00:19:57,000
No, we don't.

412
00:19:57,000 --> 00:19:58,730
The one we have works great.

413
00:19:58,740 --> 00:20:00,230
Sweetheart, you are a grown man.

414
00:20:00,240 --> 00:20:02,670
Or maybe I'm part of a new species,

415
00:20:02,670 --> 00:20:04,570
that lives for hundreds of years,

416
00:20:04,570 --> 00:20:07,280
which means I'm still basically a toddler.

417
00:20:08,580 --> 00:20:11,240
Oh, I so should have taken you to Houston.

418
00:20:14,460 --> 00:20:16,880
Does this mean you're not going to sing "Soft Kitty"?

419
00:20:16,880 --> 00:20:20,500
No, I will always sing you "Soft Kitty".

420
00:20:24,880 --> 00:20:27,140
Little ball of fur...

421
00:20:27,140 --> 00:20:28,570
Mrs. Cooper, were we supposed to take that pie

422
00:20:28,570 --> 00:20:30,650
- out of the oven? - Get out!

423
00:20:32,980 --> 00:20:33,980
Well, that was rude.

424
00:20:33,980 --> 00:20:37,220
Well, I know, but he means well.

425
00:20:38,220 --> 00:20:39,520
Sing.

426
00:20:39,520 --> 00:20:43,070
Happy kitty,sleepy kitty

427
00:20:43,080 --> 00:20:44,790
What are you trying to pull, Mom?

428
00:20:44,800 --> 00:20:47,360
From the top.

429
00:20:47,360 --> 00:20:50,830
This is what I'm talking about.

430
00:20:55,190 --> 00:20:58,610
Little ball of fur...

